SpiderFoot for Trace Labs OSINT -- relaxed dependency pins
==========================================================

THIS PACKAGE DOES NOT SHIP UPSTREAM'S DEPENDENCY PINS.

It is the only package in this repository of which that is true. The
divergence is deliberate, it was authorised, and this file is the record of
it. If you regenerate ../requirements.txt straight from upstream's
requirements.txt you will silently undo all of it and produce a package that
either does not build or does not run. Read this file first.


Why
---

Upstream's requirements.txt at the pinned commit (see debian/upstream.pin) is
a wall of 2023-era UPPER bounds. On trixie's Python 3.13 most of them are
somewhere between awkward and impossible:

  * cryptography>=3.4.8,<4 is the blocking one. No cryptography 3.x has a
    cp313 wheel, so pip would fall back to building it from source, which for
    3.x means an old Rust toolchain plus setuptools-rust. Adding rustc and
    cargo to Build-Depends was explicitly NOT authorised -- this repository
    has deferred "kind: rust" as a project of its own -- so this bound had to
    go or the package could not exist.

  * lxml>=4.9.2,<5 predates lxml's Python 3.13 support, which landed in 5.x.

  * pyOpenSSL>=21.0.0,<22 is pinned against the cryptography 3.x ABI and
    follows it.

  * The rest (CherryPy<19, Mako<2, networkx<2.7, python-whois<0.8, and so on)
    are ordinary upper bounds that upstream set and never revisited. They are
    not individually fatal, but keeping them while relaxing the fatal ones
    would produce an arbitrary half-relaxed set that nobody could reason
    about.

So: upper bounds stripped, lower bounds kept, resolved with
`uv pip compile --generate-hashes --python-version 3.13`.


The two bounds that were NOT stripped
-------------------------------------

Blanket relaxation was tried first and tested. Two packages break upstream's
code at RUNTIME -- they install fine and import fine, so nothing catches them
except actually exercising the call site:

  * PyPDF2 3.0.0 removed PdfFileReader. modules/sfp_filemeta.py line 109 calls
    PyPDF2.PdfFileReader(raw, strict=False). Under 3.0.1 that raises
    DeprecationError. The call is inside a try/except that logs and returns,
    so the failure mode is not a crash -- it is PDF metadata extraction
    silently never working, which is worse.

  * secure 1.0 removed the .framework accessor. sfwebui.py line 121 does
    secure_headers.framework.cherrypy(). Under 2.0.1 the web UI raises
    AttributeError in SpiderFootWebUi.__init__ and the server does not start
    at all.

Both retained versions (PyPDF2 1.28.6, secure 0.3.0) are pure Python and
install cleanly on 3.13, so keeping these two bounds costs nothing. Do not
"finish the job" by stripping them.


The table
---------

Upstream pin is as at commit 0f815a2 (debian/upstream.pin). Shipped version is
what ../requirements.txt actually locks. Verified means the concrete upstream
call sites were exercised against the shipped version on Python 3.13.

  upstream pin                 shipped        why
  ---------------------------- -------------- -----------------------------
  cryptography>=3.4.8,<4       50.0.1         MUST relax. No cp313 wheel for
                                              3.x; source build needs Rust,
                                              which is not authorised here.
                                              Used only via pyOpenSSL.
  lxml>=4.9.2,<5               6.1.3          MUST relax. 3.13 support is
                                              5.x+. Used as bs4's parser and
                                              by sfp_* HTML scrapers.
  pyOpenSSL>=21.0.0,<22        26.4.0         MUST relax. 21.x pins the
                                              cryptography 3.x ABI. Verified:
                                              crypto.load_certificate and
                                              SSL.Context still present.
  PyPDF2>=1.28.6,<2            1.28.6         BOUND KEPT. 3.0.0 removed
                                              PdfFileReader, which
                                              sfp_filemeta calls. Pure
                                              Python, fine on 3.13.
  secure>=0.3.0,<0.4.0         0.3.0          BOUND KEPT. 1.0 removed
                                              .framework; sfwebui calls
                                              .framework.cherrypy() at
                                              startup. Pure Python.
  networkx>=2.6.3,<2.7         3.6.1          Relaxed. spiderfoot/helpers.py
                                              imports GEXFWriter from
                                              networkx.readwrite.gexf, which
                                              still exists in 3.x. Verified
                                              via buildGraphGexf().
  netaddr>=0.8.0,<1            1.3.0          Relaxed across the 1.0 major.
                                              Verified: valid_ipv4,
                                              valid_ipv6, IPNetwork,
                                              IPAddress, AddrFormatError all
                                              still present and working.
  ExifRead>=2.3.2,<3           3.5.1          Relaxed across a major.
                                              Verified: process_file() behaves
                                              identically to 2.3.2 on a real
                                              JPEG (sfp_filemeta:148).
  publicsuffixlist>=0.9.3,<0.10 1.0.2.20260916 Relaxed. Verified:
                                              PublicSuffixList().privatesuffix
                                              (sflib.py:37). Newer list data
                                              is a feature, not a risk.
  python-whois>=0.7.3,<0.8     0.9.6          Relaxed. whois.whois() unchanged.
  ipwhois>=1.1.0,<1.2.0        1.3.0          Relaxed. IPWhois(ip).lookup_rdap
                                              unchanged.
  phonenumbers>=8.13.6,<9      9.0.39         Relaxed across a major. parse()
                                              unchanged; newer metadata is
                                              the point of the package.
  beautifulsoup4>=4.11.2,<5    4.15.0         Relaxed (no-op, 5.x not out).
                                              Verified with features="lxml".
  python-docx>=0.8.11,<0.9     1.2.0          Relaxed past 1.0. Only
                                              docx.Document() is used.
  python-pptx>=0.6.21,<0.7     1.0.2          Relaxed past 1.0. Only
                                              pptx.Presentation() is used.
  Mako>=1.2.4,<2               1.4.1          Relaxed (no-op, 2.x not out).
                                              Template/TemplateLookup verified.
  CherryPy>=18.8.0,<19         18.10.0        Relaxed (no-op, 19 not out).
  cherrypy-cors>=1.6,<2        1.7.0          Relaxed (no-op).
  requests>=2.28.2,<3          2.34.2         Relaxed (no-op, 3.x not out).
  dnspython>=2.3.0,<3          2.8.0          Relaxed (no-op, 3.x not out).
  openpyxl>=3.1.1,<4           3.1.5          Relaxed (no-op).
  pyyaml>=6.0.0,<7             6.0.3          Relaxed (no-op).
  adblockparser>=0.7,<1        0.7            Relaxed (no-op, 0.7 is latest).
  pysocks>=1.7.1,<2            1.7.1          Relaxed (no-op, latest).
  pygexf>=0.2.2,<0.3           0.2.2          Relaxed (no-op, only release).
                                              Declared by upstream but not
                                              imported anywhere in the tree;
                                              GEXF output goes through
                                              networkx. Kept to stay faithful
                                              to upstream's declared set.
  ipaddr>=2.2.0,<3             2.2.0          Relaxed (no-op, only release).
                                              Also not imported anywhere --
                                              the code uses stdlib ipaddress.
                                              Kept for the same reason.

pygexf and ipaddr are py2-era sdists with no wheels; pip builds them from
source at install time. Neither needs a compiler.


How this was verified
---------------------

Against the shipped lock, on debian:trixie / Python 3.13.5:

  * all 233 modules/sfp_*.py import cleanly (0 failures)
  * `import spiderfoot, sflib` succeeds
  * the CherryPy web UI starts and GET / returns 200
  * python3 -m compileall over the whole upstream tree is clean
  * the 14 concrete third-party call sites listed in the table above were
    exercised individually

What that does NOT prove is in "Known limits" below.


Known limits
------------

Import success is not scan success. Each of the 233 modules talks to a
different third-party service, and the great majority of them require an API
key that this build has none of. So:

  * Every module was verified to IMPORT and to byte-compile under 3.13. That
    catches syntax-level and import-level breakage from the relaxed pins,
    which is the failure class relaxation actually creates.

  * NO module was verified to complete a live scan. Modules whose network
    behaviour depends on a relaxed library -- anything parsing HTML through
    lxml/bs4, anything doing TLS through pyOpenSSL/cryptography, sfp_whois and
    sfp_dnsresolve through python-whois/dnspython/netaddr -- are the ones to
    look at first if a scan misbehaves, because those are the libraries whose
    majors moved furthest.

  * sfp_filemeta's PDF path is the one place where a relaxed pin was actively
    reverted rather than merely tested. If PyPDF2 is ever unpinned, that is
    where it breaks, quietly.

If you are chasing a runtime bug that smells like a library API change, the
first diagnostic is to compare against upstream's own pins in a scratch venv
on an older Python, not to re-pin anything here.


Regenerating the lock
---------------------

  1. Read debian/upstream.pin and confirm the commit you are packaging.
  2. Take upstream's requirements.txt at that commit.
  3. Strip the upper bounds, keep the lower bounds, and RE-APPLY the two kept
     bounds above unless you have re-tested them.
  4. uv pip compile --generate-hashes --no-header --python-version 3.13
  5. Re-prepend the provenance header and the --require-hashes line.
  6. Re-run the verification listed above, especially the web UI 200 check --
     it is the one that catches a broken secure/CherryPy combination.
  7. Update this table.
